er medium-effort policy; the current skill defaults to Sol/high, Terra for bounded work, and Luna only for tightly specified mechanical work. Blind-graded like the rest:\\n/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/RESULTS.md:138:_The page's charts cover the Anthropic arms (fable-orchestrate, opus-orchestrate, and the Claude advisor consult), run identically on every brief: the two orchestrators get the brief verbatim in a fresh headless session, and the advisor arm always runs the same three scripted steps (plain solve, one consult, plain revise). The Codex-led 46-orchestrate arm was re-run on 2026-07-12 at the owner's direction — headless `codex exec`, gpt-5.6-terra · medium, all five briefs, same protocol and rubrics — and its results are integrated below and summarized on the page; it stays out of the USD charts because Codex reports tokens, not dollars. The superseded 2026-07-11 46 captures are in git history. The advisor-Codex arm remains deferred._\\n/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/RESULTS.md:172:| fable-orchestrate | **Pass (4/6).** A ✓ (Type-of-Place 0.158→0.115 rank-flip), B ✓ (binary ±25.1 invariant), C ✓ (MM .626/.374; crime .251 > commute .237 > housing .198), E ✓ (avoids \\\"dominant\\\"). Misses D — does not flag the ~1.4pp crime-commute gap as within noise; corrected magnitude only. | Okabe-Ito, 300 dpi, caption-not-title (two facets, vline at 0). | 875k in / 12.7k out · $4.13 | 790 s (13.2 min), 21 turns | 2 → Opus deep-reasoner (sensitivity, effort max) + blind gpt-5.6-terra @ xhigh Codex peer (parallel). Lead wrote memo. | One exploratory shell probe for `codex-peer.sh`. |\\n/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/RESULTS.md:176:| advisor arm (Codex) | **Pass** (revised memo). B ✓, C ✓, D ✓ (flags crime not formally first, \\\"comparable to commuting time\\\"; no formal test run), E ✓. A thin (concedes via the invariant range rather than a shifting-AMCE number); corrected magnitude only. Consult caught an overclaim (\\\"largest span\\\" untested → softened) **and** an over-concession (range is reference-invariant — say so), fixed CI formatting, defined the comparison set. | 300 dpi, caption-not-title, base-R — **not Okabe-Ito** (custom teal/grey). | step1 115,348 + step3 111,520 tok | ~8 min (2 steps) | 1 consult (sol-advisor, gpt-5.6-terra · medium). Solve → consult → revise. | Step-1 solver's own advisor attempt blocked (nested advisory under the sandbox). |\\n/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/RESULTS.md:222:**Product divergence — different answers.** (1) The estimand splits at T2, and it persists across re-runs. fable and opus lead with IRR-corrected AMCEs (crime −25.1pp, projoint default). 46 leads with uncorrected AMCEs (crime −16.5pp), labeled — in the re-run, as the outcome of an explicit integration decision after its two subagents split on exactly this. A reader takes away 25pp from two modes and 16pp from the third. (2) The original 46/T3 capture's marginal means were simply wrong (.541/.458 vs reference .626/.374); the 2026-07-12 re-run reproduces the reference MMs exactly and discloses tau = 0.172, with the fix traceable to the run's own estimand discipline (\\\"one explicitly selected, consistent estimand\\\"). The wrong-MM finding stands as evidence about single-draw risk, not about the current artifact. (3) opus/T3's decorrelated peer was uncalibrated. The lead called `codex exec` without a `--model` pin, so the second opinion ran at the machine's gpt-5.5 default rather than the prescribed gpt-5.6-terra@xhigh. The memo is still the strongest inline product, so the deviation cost process fidelity, not accuracy. The figure conventions mostly converged on re-run: the new 46/T3 figure is base-R with genuine Okabe-Ito hex colors (the old custom teal is gone); advisor-Codex's teal figure stands pending that arm's re-run