-Never share: 86.2774% (contribution -0.035149)\\nClean Earlier-vs-Later/not-yet share: 8.3301%\\nBacon reconstruction: -0.03654894; TWFE coefficient: -0.03654894\\n\\nDynamic Callaway-Sant'Anna estimates:\\n event_time   estimate std_error    conf_low   conf_high\\n         -3  0.0250218 0.0181189 -0.01049060  0.06053426\\n         -2  0.0244587 0.0142364 -0.00344409  0.05236158\\n         -1  0.0000000        NA          NA          NA\\n          0 -0.0199318 0.0118264 -0.04311106  0.00324743\\n          1 -0.0509574 0.0168935 -0.08406797 -0.01784676\\n          2 -0.1372587 0.0364357 -0.20867133 -0.06584615\\n          3 -0.1008114 0.0343592 -0.16815421 -0.03346852\\nOmnibus pre-treatment Wald-test p-value: 0.168120\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./run.jsonl:55:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The estimates reconcile tightly: TWFE is −0.0365 log points, both Callaway–Sant’Anna specifications and Sun–Abraham are essentially −0.0400, and Roth–Sant’Anna is −0.0471. The Bacon decomposition reconstructs TWFE exactly; 5.39% of weight is in later-vs-earlier comparisons versus 86.28% in treated-vs-never comparisons. I’m checking the figure and then will integrate the two blind reviews before drafting the memo.\"}}\n./run.jsonl-56-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' estimates-table.md; file figures/event-study.png; sips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight -g dpiWidth -g dpiHeight figures/event-study.png 2>/dev/null; wc -c figures/event-study.png; ps -p 24196 -o pid=,stat=,etime=,command= 2>/dev/null || true; for f in terra-review.log claude-fable-review.log; do printf '%s bytes=' \\\\\\\"\\\"'$f\\\"; wc -c < \\\"$f\\\"; done'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n./run.jsonl:57:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_32\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' estimates-table.md; file figures/event-study.png; sips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight -g dpiWidth -g dpiHeight figures/event-study.png 2>/dev/null; wc -c figures/event-study.png; ps -p 24196 -o pid=,stat=,etime=,command= 2>/dev/null || true; for f in terra-review.log claude-fable-review.log; do printf '%s bytes=' \\\\\\\"\\\"'$f\\\"; wc -c < \\\"$f\\\"; done'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# ATT estimates\\n\\nAll effects are in log points of teen employment.\\n\\n| Estimator | ATT | Standard error | Control/comparison group |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| Naive TWFE | -0.0365 | 0.0133 | Pooled TWFE; already-treated counties may act as controls |\\n| Callaway-Sant'Anna | -0.0398 | 0.0121 | Never-treated and not-yet-treated counties |\\n| Callaway-Sant'Anna | -0.0400 | 0.0120 | Never-treated counties only |\\n| Sun-Abraham | -0.0400 | 0.0118 | Never-treated reference cohort; cohort x event-time interactions |\\n| Roth-Sant'Anna (staggered) | -0.0471 | 0.0116 | Not-yet-/never-treated cohorts; native efficient adjustment |\\n\\n*Notes:* TWFE and Sun-Abraham standard errors are clustered by county. Callaway-Sant'Anna uses analytical county-level clustered inference. Roth-Sant'Anna reports `staggered`'s native unit-level standard error. All estimators are unweighted and include no covariates.\\nfigures/event-study.png: PNG image data, 2240 x 1440, 8-bit/color RGB, non-interlaced\\n/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/extreme-stagdid/figures/event-study.png\\n  pixelWidth: 2240\\n  pixelHeight: 1440\\n  dpiWidth: 320.000\\n  dpiHeight: 320.000\\n   88209 figures/event-study.png\\nterra-review.log bytes=  910469\\nclaude-fable-review.log bytes=       0\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./run.jsonl-58-{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"tail -n 100 terra-review.log; ls -l terra-review.log claude-fable-review.log; pgrep -af 'codex exec|claude-peer|claude -p' | sed -n '1,120p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n./run.jsonl:59:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"tail -n 100 terra-review.log; ls -l terra-review.log claude-fable-review.log; pgrep -af 'codex exec|claude-peer|claude -p' | sed -n '1,120p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# estimate group-time average treatment effects without covariates\\nmw.attgt <- att_gt(yname = \\\"lemp\\\",\\n                   gname = \\\"first.treat\\\",\\n                   idname = \\\"countyreal\\\",\\n                   tname = \\\"year\\\",\\n                   xformla = ~1,\\n                   data = mpdta\\n                   )\\n\\n# summarize the results\\nsummary(mw.attgt)\\n\\n# plot the results\\n# set ylim so that all plots have the same scale along y-axis\\nggdid(mw.attgt, ylim = c(-.3, .3))\\n```\\n\\nThere are a few things to notice in this case\\n\\n* There does not appear to be much evidence against the parallel trends assumption.  One fails to reject using the Wald test reported in `summary`; likewise the uniform confidence bands cover 0 in all pre-treatment periods.\\n\\n* There is some evidence of negative effects of the minimum wage on employment.  Two group-time average treatment effects are negative and statistically different from 0.  These results also suggest that it may be helpful to aggregate the group-time average treatment effects.\\n\\n```{r,  fig.width=8,fig.height=5, fig.align='center', out.width=\\\"90%\\\", dpi = 200}\\n# aggregate the group-time average treatment effects\\nmw.dyn <- aggte(mw.attgt, type = \\\"dynamic\\\")\\nsummary(mw.dyn)\\nggdid(mw.dyn, ylim = c(-.3, .3))\\n```\\n\\nThese continue to be simultaneous confidence bands for dynamic effects. The results are broadly similar to the ones from the group-time average treatment effects:  one fails to reject parallel trends in pre-treatment periods and it looks like somewhat negative effects of the minimum wage on youth employment.\\n\\nOne potential issue with these dynamic effect estimators is that the composition of the groups changes with different lengths of exposure in the event study plots.  For example, for the group of states who increased their minimum wage in 2007, we can only identify the instantaneous average effect of the minimum wage ($e=0$), whereas for states that raised their  minimum wage in 2004 (2006), we can identify the average effect of the minimum wage on event-times $e=0,1,2,3$ ($e=0,1$). When computing the event-study plot for $e=0$, we would aggregate the effects for all three groups, but this is not the case when $e=1,2,3$. If the effects of the minimum wage are systematically different across groups (here, there is not much evidence of this as the effect for all groups seems to be close to 0 on impact and perhaps becoming more negative over time), then this can lead to confounding dynamics and selective treatment timing among different groups.  \\n\\nOne way to combat this is to balance the sample by (i) only including groups that are exposed to the treatment for at least a certain number of time periods and (ii) only look at dynamic effects in those time periods.  In the `did` package, one can do this by specifying the `balance_e` option.  Here, we set `balance_e = 1` -- what this does is to only consider groups of states that\\nare treated in 2004 and 2006 (so we can compute event-study-type parameters for them for $e=0,1$), drops the group treated in 2007 (as we can not compute the event-study-type parameter with $e=1$ for this group)), and then only looks at instantaneous average effects and the average effect one period after states raised the minimum wage.\\n\\n```{r,  fig.width=8,fig.height=5, fig.align='center', out.width=\\\"90%\\\", dpi = 200}\\nmw.dyn.balance <- aggte(mw.attgt, type = \\\"dynamic\\\", balance_e = 1)\\nsummary(mw.dyn.balance)\\nggdid(mw.dyn.balance, ylim = c(-.3, .3))\\n```\\n\\nFinally, we can run all the same results including a covariate.  In this application the results turn out to be nearly identical, and here we provide just the code for estimating the group-time average treatment effects while including covariates.  The other steps are otherwise the same.\\n\\n```{r eval=FALSE}\\nmw.attgt.X <- att_gt(yname = \\\"lemp\\\",\\n                     gname = \\\"first.treat\\\",\\n                     idname = \\\"countyreal\\\",\\n                     tname = \\\"year\\\",\\n* There does not appear to be much evidence against the parallel trends assumption.  One fails to reject using the Wald test reported in `summary`; likewise the uniform confidence bands cover 0 in all pre-treatment periods.\\n\\n* There is some evidence of negative effects of the minimum wage on employment.  Two group-time average treatment effects are negative and statistically different from 0.  These results also suggest that it may be helpful to aggregate the group-time average treatment effects.\\n\\n```{r,  fig.width=8,fig.height=5, fig.align='center', out.width=\\\"90%\\\", dpi = 200}\\n# aggregate the group-time average treatment effects\\nmw.dyn <- aggte(mw.attgt, type = \\\"dynamic\\\")\\nsummary(mw.dyn)\\nggdid(mw.dyn, ylim = c(-.3, .3))\\n```\\n\\nThese continue to be simultaneous confidence bands for dynamic effects. The results are broadly similar to the ones from the group-time average treatment effects:  one fails to reject parallel trends in pre-treatment periods and it looks like somewhat negative effects of the minimum wage on youth employment.\\n\\nOne potential issue with these dynamic effect estimators is that the composition of the groups changes with different lengths of exposure in the event study plots.  For example, for the group of states who increased their minimum wage in 2007, we can only identify the instantaneous average effect of the minimum wage ($e=0$), whereas for states that raised their  minimum wage in 2004 (2006), we can identify the average effect of the minimum wage on event-times $e=0,1,2,3$ ($e=0,1$). When computing the event-study plot for $e=0$, we would aggregate the effects for all three groups, but this is not the case when $e=1,2,3$. If the effects of the minimum wage are systematically different across groups (here, there is not much evidence of this as the effect for all groups seems to be close to 0 on impact and perhaps becoming more negative over time), then this can lead to confounding dynamics and selective treatment timing among different groups.  \\n\\nOne way to combat this is to balance the sample by (i) only including groups that are exposed to the treatment for at least a certain number of time periods and (ii) only look at dynamic effects in those time periods.  In the `did` package, one can do this by specifying the `balance_e` option.  Here, we set `balance_e = 1` -- what this does is to only consider groups of states that\\nare treated in 2004 and 2006 (so we can compute event-study-type parameters for them for $e=0,1$), drops the group treated in 2007 (as we can not compute the event-study-type parameter with $e=1$ for this group)), and then only looks at instantaneous average effects and the average effect one period after states raised the minimum wage.\\n\\n```{r,  fig.width=8,fig.height=5, fig.align='center', out.width=\\\"90%\\\", dpi = 200}\\nmw.dyn.balance <- aggte(mw.attgt, type = \\\"dynamic\\\", balance_e = 1)\\nsummary(mw.dyn.balance)\\nggdid(mw.dyn.balance, ylim = c(-.3, .3))\\n```\\n\\n\\nexec\\n/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -a -n -C 3 'staggered\\\\\\\\(|estimand|se_neyman|never treated|Inf' /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/staggered/help/staggered.rdb /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/staggered/R/staggered.rdb | head -200\\nrg -a -n -C 3 'sunab\\\\\\\\(|never treated|cohort.*period|Inf' /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/fixest/help/fixest.rdb /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/fixest/R/fixest.rdb | head -220\\nrg -a -n -C 3 'control_group|notyettreated|nevertreated|overall.att|overall.se|att.egt|se.egt' /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/help/did.rdb /opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/R/did.rdb | head -200\\\" in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/extreme-stagdid\\n succeeded in 0ms:\\nexec\\n/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files -g '\\\"'!BRIEF.md'\\\"' . | sed -n '1,160p'\\nfind . -maxdepth 2 -type f -print | sed -n '1,160p'\\\" in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/extreme-stagdid\\n succeeded in 0ms:\\n./run.jsonl\\n./terra-review.log\\n./claude-fable-review.log\\n./figures/event-study.png\\n./estimates-table.md\\n./script.R\\n./peer-review-prompt.md\\n./peer-review-prompt.md\\n./script.R\\n./estimates-table.md\\n./figures/event-study.png\\n./claude-fable-review.log\\n./BRIEF.md\\n./terra-review.log\\n./run.jsonl\\n\\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 scdenney  staff       0 Jul 19 10:13 claude-fable-review.log\\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 scdenney  staff  926531 Jul 19 10:15 terra-review.log\\n38154\\n38156\\n38157\\n38304\\n38306\\n38307\\n38461\\n38463\\n38465\\n38629\\n38631\\n38633\\n38902\\n38904\\n38905\\n39095\\n39097\\n39102\\n40007\\n40010\\n40011\\n40012\\n41817\\n41819\\n42639\\n42640\\n42901\\n42902\\n42903\\n42905\\n42907\\n42908\\n44017\\n44019\\n44227\\n44229\\n44231\\n44345\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n  att = c(\n    twfe_table[\"treated\", \"Estimate\"],\n    cs_notyet$simple$overall.att,\n    cs_never$simple$overall.att,\n    sunab_table[\"ATT\", \"Estimate\"],\n    staggered_att$estimate[[1]]\n  ),\n  std_error = c(\n    twfe_table[\"treated\", \"Std. Error\"],\n    cs_notyet$simple$overall.se,\n    cs_never$simple$overall.se,\n    sunab_table[\"ATT\", \"Std. Error\"],\n    staggered_att$se[[1]]\n  ),\n  comparison_group = c(\n    \"Pooled TWFE; already-treated counties may act as controls\",\n    \"Never-treated and not-yet-treated counties\",\n    \"Never-treated counties only\",\n    \"Never-treated reference cohort; cohort x event-time interactions\",\n    \"Not-yet-/never-treated cohorts; native efficient adjustment\"\n  ),\n  stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\n\ntable_lines <- c(\n  \"# ATT estimates\",\n  \"\",\n  \"All effects are in log points of teen employment.\",\n  \"\",\n  \"| Estimator | ATT | Standard error | Control/comparison group |\",\n  \"|---|---:|---:|---|\",\n  sprintf(\n    \"| %s | %.4f | %.4f | %s |\",\n    estimates$estimator,\n    estimates$att,\n    estimates$std_error,\n    estimates$comparison_group\n  ),\n  \"\",\n  paste0(\n    \"*Notes:* TWFE and Sun-Abraham standard errors are clustered by county. \",\n    \"Callaway-Sant'Anna uses analytical county-level clustered inference. \",\n    \"Roth-Sant'Anna reports `staggered`'s native unit-level standard error. \",\n    \"All estimators are unweighted and include no covariates.\"\n  )\n)\nwriteLines(table_lines, \"estimates-table.md\")\n\n# Console diagnostics make the key reconciliation directly auditable.\ncat(\"\\nATT estimates (log points):\\n\")\nprint(estimates, row.names = FALSE, digits = 6)\ncat(\"\\nGoodman-Bacon weight shares and weighted contributions:\\n\")\nprint(bacon_by_type, row.names = FALSE, digits = 7)\ncat(sprintf(\n  paste0(\n    \"\\nPotentially contaminated Later-vs-Earlier share: %.4f%% \",\n    \"(contribution %.6f)\\n\"\n  ),\n  100 * dangerous_share,\n  dangerous_contribution\n))\ncat(sprintf(\n  paste0(\n    \"Clean Treated-vs-Never share: %.4f%% (contribution %.6f)\\n\",\n    \"Clean Earlier-vs-Later/not-yet share: %.4f%%\\n\"\n  ),\n  100 * clean_never_share,\n  clean_never_contribution,\n  100 * clean_notyet_share\n))\ncat(sprintf(\n  \"Bacon reconstruction: %.8f; TWFE coefficient: %.8f\\n\",\n  sum(bacon_result$weight * bacon_result$estimate),\n  coef(twfe_fit)[[\"treated\"]]\n))\ncat(\"\\nDynamic Callaway-Sant'Anna estimates:\\n\")\nprint(event_study, row.names = FALSE, digits = 6)\ncat(sprintf(\n  \"Omnibus pre-treatment Wald-test p-value: %.6f\\n\",\n  as.numeric(cs_never$mp$Wpval)\n))\n\ncodex\n## Conclusion\n\nUse Callaway–Sant’Anna with `control_group = \"nevertreated\"` as the primary no-covariate ATT, and report `\"notyettreated\"` plus Sun–Abraham as robustness checks. Do not make Roth–Sant’Anna primary here: `staggered` is designed for randomized staggered rollouts, a stronger design assumption than the other estimators require.\n\n## Checkable evidence\n\n- `did` 2.5.1’s local vignette explicitly states that never-treated units’ `gname` must be `0`; use `mpdta$first.treat` unchanged. See `did/doc/did-basics.Rmd`, lines 256–264.\n- `fixest::sunab(first.treat, year)` can also use the unchanged `0`: its local walkthrough uses an out-of-period cohort code for never-treated units and explains `sunab(cohort, period)` aggregation. Here `0` lies outside observed years 2003–2007. See `fixest/doc/fixest_walkthrough.Rmd`, lines 628–660 and 681–688. Do not recode it to `Inf`.\n- `staggered::staggered` requires `g = Inf` for never-treated units. Thus use `ifelse(first.treat == 0, Inf, first.treat)`. This is the only required sentinel recode.\n- The `did` vignette’s worked `mpdta` specification confirms `lemp`, `countyreal`, `year`, `first.treat`, and `xformla = ~1`; it also cautions that dynamic aggregation changes cohort composition over event time. `did/doc/did-basics.Rmd`, lines 277–316.\n- `bacondecomp` documents reconstruction as `sum(estimate * weight)`, which must equal the FE coefficient in the no-controls case. See `bacondecomp/doc/bacon.R`, lines 8–18.\n\n## Implementation recommendations\n\n```r\nD <- as.integer(mpdta$first.treat > 0 & mpdta$year >= mpdta$first.treat)\n\ntwfe <- fixest::feols(lemp ~ D | countyreal + year,\n                      data = mpdta, vcov = ~countyreal)\n# coef(twfe)[\"D\"]; fixest::se(twfe)[\"D\"]\n```\n\nFor each `cg` in `c(\"nevertreated\", \"notyettreated\")`:\n\n```r\nmp <- did::att_gt(\"lemp\", \"year\", \"countyreal\", \"first.treat\",\n                  xformla = ~1, data = mpdta,\n                  control_group = cg, bstrap = TRUE,\n                  biters = 999, clustervars = \"countyreal\")\nsimple <- did::aggte(mp, type = \"simple\")\n# simple$overall.att; simple$overall.se\n```\n\nUse county-clustered bootstrap inference consistently for C&S; `notyettreated` uses never- plus still-untreated counties at each date, whereas `nevertreated` uses only never-treated counties.\n\n```r\nsa <- fixest::feols(lemp ~ fixest::sunab(first.treat, year) |\n                    countyreal + year,\n                    data = mpdta, vcov = ~countyreal)\nsa_att <- summary(sa, agg = \"ATT\")\n# coeftable(sa_att)[\"ATT\", c(\"Estimate\", \"Std. Error\")]\n```\n\nFor Bacon, aggregate `weight` and `weight * estimate` by `type`; verify both weights sum to one and contributions sum to TWFE. “Later vs Earlier Treated” is the contaminated comparison category. Its reported Bacon `weight` is a nonnegative decomposition share; the negative-weight critique instead concerns TWFE’s implicit weights on underlying group-time treatment effects. Do not describe a negative Bacon share as evidence of the critique.\n\n## Interpretation guardrails\n\nA defensible diagnostic is:\n\n```r\ndyn <- did::aggte(mp_never, type = \"dynamic\", min_e = -3, max_e = 1)\n```\n\nShow leads `e = -3, -2`, with `e = -1` as the normalized reference, and simultaneous bootstrap bands. Report the pre-treatment Wald test from the C&S object as supplementary evidence. Failure to reject supports compatibility with pre-period parallel trends; it does not establish parallel trends or rule out low-powered violations.\n\nConclude that the TWFE critique materially changes this dataset’s conclusion only if: the later-vs-earlier Bacon share/contribution is consequential, and heterogeneity-robust ATT estimates differ enough from TWFE to change sign, practical magnitude, or inferential/substantive conclusion. Similar estimates alone support robustness for this dataset, not a general endorsement of TWFE.\n\n## Residual risk\n\nI could not run an independent R calculation because this read-only session prevents R from creating its required temporary directory. I therefore provide no independently computed numerical cross-check and would require a fresh read-only R run before endorsing any existing reported estimates or Bacon shares.\ntokens used\n82,351\n## Conclusion\n\nUse Callaway–Sant’Anna with `control_group = \"nevertreated\"` as the primary no-covariate ATT, and report `\"notyettreated\"` plus Sun–Abraham as robustness checks. Do not make Roth–Sant’Anna primary here: `staggered` is designed for randomized staggered rollouts, a stronger design assumption than the other estimators require.\n\n## Checkable evidence\n\n- `did` 2.5.1’s local vignette explicitly states that never-treated units’ `gname` must be `0`; use `mpdta$first.treat` unchanged. See `did/doc/did-basics.Rmd`, lines 256–264.\n- `fixest::sunab(first.treat, year)` can also use the unchanged `0`: its local walkthrough uses an out-of-period cohort code for never-treated units and explains `sunab(cohort, period)` aggregation. Here `0` lies outside observed years 2003–2007. See `fixest/doc/fixest_walkthrough.Rmd`, lines 628–660 and 681–688. Do not recode it to `Inf`.\n- `staggered::staggered` requires `g = Inf` for never-treated units. Thus use `ifelse(first.treat == 0, Inf, first.treat)`. This is the only required sentinel recode.\n- The `did` vignette’s worked `mpdta` specification confirms `lemp`, `countyreal`, `year`, `first.treat`, and `xformla = ~1`; it also cautions that dynamic aggregation changes cohort composition over event time. `did/doc/did-basics.Rmd`, lines 277–316.\n- `bacondecomp` documents reconstruction as `sum(estimate * weight)`, which must equal the FE coefficient in the no-controls case. See `bacondecomp/doc/bacon.R`, lines 8–18.\n\n## Implementation recommendations\n\n```r\nD <- as.integer(mpdta$first.treat > 0 & mpdta$year >= mpdta$first.treat)\n\ntwfe <- fixest::feols(lemp ~ D | countyreal + year,\n                      data = mpdta, vcov = ~countyreal)\n# coef(twfe)[\"D\"]; fixest::se(twfe)[\"D\"]\n```\n\nFor each `cg` in `c(\"nevertreated\", \"notyettreated\")`:\n\n```r\nmp <- did::att_gt(\"lemp\", \"year\", \"countyreal\", \"first.treat\",\n                  xformla = ~1, data = mpdta,\n                  control_group = cg, bstrap = TRUE,\n                  biters = 999, clustervars = \"countyreal\")\nsimple <- did::aggte(mp, type = \"simple\")\n# simple$overall.att; simple$overall.se\n```\n\nUse county-clustered bootstrap inference consistently for C&S; `notyettreated` uses never- plus still-untreated counties at each date, whereas `nevertreated` uses only never-treated counties.\n\n```r\nsa <- fixest::feols(lemp ~ fixest::sunab(first.treat, year) |\n                    countyreal + year,\n                    data = mpdta, vcov = ~countyreal)\nsa_att <- summary(sa, agg = \"ATT\")\n# coeftable(sa_att)[\"ATT\", c(\"Estimate\", \"Std. Error\")]\n```\n\nFor Bacon, aggregate `weight` and `weight * estimate` by `type`; verify both weights sum to one and contributions sum to TWFE. “Later vs Earlier Treated” is the contaminated comparison category. Its reported Bacon `weight` is a nonnegative decomposition share; the negative-weight critique instead concerns TWFE’s implicit weights on underlying group-time treatment effects. Do not describe a negative Bacon share as evidence of the critique.\n\n## Interpretation guardrails\n\nA defensible diagnostic is:\n\n```r\ndyn <- did::aggte(mp_never, type = \"dynamic\", min_e = -3, max_e = 1)\n```\n\nShow leads `e = -3, -2`, with `e = -1` as the normalized reference, and simultaneous bootstrap bands. Report the pre-treatment Wald test from the C&S object as supplementary evidence. Failure to reject supports compatibility with pre-period parallel trends; it does not establish parallel trends or rule out low-powered violations.\n\nConclude that the TWFE critique materially changes this dataset’s conclusion only if: the later-vs-earlier Bacon share/contribution is consequential, and heterogeneity-robust ATT estimates differ enough from TWFE to change sign, practical magnitude, or inferential/substantive conclusion. Similar estimates alone support robustness for this dataset, not a general endorsement of TWFE.\n\n## Residual risk\n\nI could not run an independent R calculation because this read-only session prevents R from creating its required temporary directory. I therefore provide no independently computed numerical cross-check and would require a fresh read-only R run before endorsing any existing reported estimates or Bacon shares.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
